The BCM4361 (the Galaxy S9's radio) in Linux 7.0.14's brcmfmac, the two places where the kernel's
brcmfmac=N stops the driver (wifi/lx/brcmfmac/lx_brcmf.c), the one where brcmf_attach() is through and the
station above it (lx_brcmf.c, lx_cfg.c) may begin, and the one where the probe, before it asks for the firmware, lets
lx_brcmf_identify() read which module the chip is from its OTP (through two words' access to the backplane,
lx_brcmf_bp_read32() and _write32(), which are pcie.c's own buscore accessors). ISC, as the files it changes.

Facts from Samsung's bcmdhd for the S9 (GPL-2.0; read for these facts only, no code taken), kernel e5906667068e,
drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/bcmdhd_101_12: include/bcmdevs_legacy.h (chip 0x4361, PCI device 0x441f),
dhd_linux.c dhd_validate_chipid() (the chip may say 0x4347 instead), dhd_pcie.c dhdpcie_dongle_attach()'s RAM base
(CR4_4347_RAM_BASE for both) and include/sbchipc.h (0x170000), dhd_custom_cis.c (revisions: B0 3, B1 4, B2 5;
the S9's firmware is _b0 or _b2; dhd_parse_board_information_bcm(): the module's CIS in OTP).

--- a/brcmfmac/chip.c
+++ b/brcmfmac/chip.c
@@ -741,6 +741,8 @@
 	case BRCM_CC_43751_CHIP_ID:
 	case BRCM_CC_43752_CHIP_ID:
 	case BRCM_CC_4377_CHIP_ID:
+	case BRCM_CC_4347_CHIP_ID:	/* (HakiOS: the BCM4361, as Broadcom's own driver has it) */
+	case BRCM_CC_4361_CHIP_ID:
 		return 0x170000;
 	case BRCM_CC_4378_CHIP_ID:
 		return 0x352000;
